[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Concepts (/docs/pr-preview/pr-56/platform/updates/concepts)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The terms Lamassu Updates uses, and how they fit together. Read this page before the guides if \"update pack\", \"launch\" or \"module\" is new to you.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Vocabulary [#vocabulary]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The dashboard and this documentation say **update pack**. The REST API and the code say **distribution set** (`/distribution-sets/...`). They are the same thing.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Term            | Meaning                                                                                                                                    | API / code name        |\n  | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------- |\n  | Update pack     | A named, versioned deliverable you send to devices                                                                                         | `distribution-set`     |\n  | Software module | One typed, named component inside a pack, such as an application. Identified by the key `type:name`, where `type` is `os` or `application` | `softwaremodule`       |\n  | Artifact        | A file (binary) attached to a module or pack                                                                                               | `artifact`             |\n  | Device group    | A set of devices registered in Lamassu IoT. A pack belongs to a group                                                                      | `groupID`              |\n  | Launch campaign | One rollout of one pack version to the devices of a group                                                                                  | `launch`               |\n  | Job             | One device's update inside a launch                                                                                                        | job, device assignment |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Update packs [#update-packs]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A pack has a **version** (semantic, `x.y.z`) and a **status**:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Status         | Meaning                                                                                              |\n  | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `draft`        | Editable. Artifacts can be added or replaced. Not built yet.                                         |\n  | `built`        | The deliverable exists. The version is immutable. Create a new version to change it.                 |\n  | `build_failed` | A build was attempted and failed. Still editable and retryable. The reason is in `last_build_error`. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A pack has one **packaging**:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Packaging | Delivered as                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Workflow terminal state |\n  | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n  | `swu`     | A `.swu` built with swugenerator, optionally signed and encrypted                                                                                                                                                                     | `ACTIVATED`             |\n  | `non-swu` | The raw artifacts. Native mode builds a `.tar.gz` package (optionally encrypted, `.tar.gz.enc`). hawkBit mode delivers a `raw` module's single file as uploaded (optionally encrypted), and archives several files as `<name>.tar.gz` | `INSTALLED`             |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A pack can also carry **preconditions**: rules such as \"the device must already have pack X at version 1.2.0 or higher\", or \"the module `os:gateway-fw` at version 2.1.0 or higher\". Each precondition names exactly one pack (`required_distribution_set_name`) or one module (`required_module_key`) and a semantic `min_version`. A device that fails a precondition is not deployable, unless an operator forces the launch. Each launch records which devices failed and why.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Software modules [#software-modules]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A pack is a composition of modules. A pack holds one `os` module (the mandatory anchor) and any number of `application` modules. In native mode a pack without module rows is shown with one implicit module `os:<pack name>`, and the files you upload to the pack belong to it. A module holds its own files and, optionally, release notes. You can:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * create a module on its own in the **catalog** (`POST /updates/v1/softwaremodules`) and compose packs from it later,\n  * add a module to a pack directly, or\n  * import a module that another pack already has.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A module's **delivery intent** says how it will ship: `undecided` (default), `swu-build`, `swu-prebuilt` or `raw`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  How modules relate to the build differs by backend:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * **native**: modules are *build inputs*. The pack builds one `.swu` from all of them, and a launch sends each device a single download address.\n  * **hawkbit**: each module has its *own deliverable*, built in the module catalog before the pack exists. Only built modules can be linked into a pack, and a linked module is frozen. hawkBit hands every module's artifacts to the device, so a pack with several modules installs as several independent SWUpdate transactions. SWUpdate cannot roll back across several `.swu` files.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Importing a module also differs. In hawkBit mode the import **links** it, so both packs share the module and a rebuild is seen by both. In native mode it **copies** it, so the two diverge afterwards. The listing of reusable modules tells you which one you will get (`shared`).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Launch campaigns [#launch-campaigns]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A launch rolls one pack version out to a group's devices in **batches**.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Strategy [#strategy]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Field                 | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n  | --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `rollout_type`        | How a batch is sized. Only `percentage` makes `rollout_value` a percentage. Any other value (`fixed`, or `numeric`, which the dashboard sends) makes it a device count                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n  | `rollout_value`       | The percentage, or the device count. Must be greater than 0                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n  | `workflow_type`       | The WFX workflow: `direct` (no confirmation), `phased` (confirmation steps) or `download-install`. Use `download-install` for `non-swu` packs. Any other value is passed to WFX as a full workflow name, such as `wfx.workflow.dau.simple`. In hawkBit mode there is no WFX: `phased` becomes a hawkBit soft action (the device confirms or postpones) and every other value a forced action |\n  | `test_device_id`      | A canary. The first batch is this one device, and it must succeed before anyone else is touched                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n  | `auto`                | Release the next batch automatically when conditions are met                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `approval_threshold`  | % of the current batch that must succeed before the next batch starts (0 to 100). Unset, 0 or 100 means the whole batch                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n  | `error_threshold`     | The launch is cancelled once this % of all devices have failed (rounded up, so 10% of 100 devices cancels at the 10th failure). `0` means no limit                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n  | `scheduled_at`        | Planned start time (RFC 3339). Native mode starts the first batch when it passes (polled every 30 seconds); hawkBit mode hands it to hawkBit as the rollout's start time                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n  | `name`, `description` | Display name (defaults to the pack name) and free-text notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `weight`              | A stored priority hint, 0 to 1000. Nothing reads it                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Lifecycle [#lifecycle]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A launch is `running`, `paused` (resumable), `cancelled` (terminal) or `completed` (terminal). A device inside it goes `pending` → `active` → `completed` or `failed`. You can retry only the failed devices (`retry-failed`). In native mode this also reopens a finished or cancelled launch. The operator controls are `rollout`, `pause`, `resume`, `cancel`, `complete` and `retry-failed`, and the strategy can be edited while the launch exists. Native mode supports all of them. hawkBit mode has no `complete` and no strategy edit, because a rollout's groups and thresholds are fixed at creation (see [Capabilities](/docs/platform/updates/reference/capabilities)).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Versions, compliance and inventory [#versions-compliance-and-inventory]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Beyond one-off launches you can declare the **latest version** of a pack for a group. The service then reports **drift**: devices that are not on the latest version. You can force one device to the latest version, or to an exact one. See the [API reference](/docs/platform/updates/reference/api).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The service also keeps a **device inventory**: which pack version each device carries now, with the artifacts that version's manifest declares, and the history of update attempts. Both are filled as the service's launches run.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  How an update reaches a device (native mode) [#how-an-update-reaches-a-device-native-mode]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```mermaid\n  flowchart TB\n    op[\"Operator builds the pack\"] --> launch[\"Launch creates a WFX job per device\"]\n    launch --> poll[\"Device polls WFX\"]\n    poll --> dl[\"Device downloads the .swu from update_domain\"]\n    dl --> inst[\"Device installs and reports each state to WFX\"]\n    inst --> cnt[\"Launch counters update\"]\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The device-side state machine is a WFX workflow, in `wfx/workflow/dau/` of the repository:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Workflow file                           | States                                                                 | Use                                                                                  |\n  | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n  | `wfx.workflow.dau.direct.yml`           | INSTALL → INSTALLING → INSTALLED → ACTIVATE → ACTIVATING → ACTIVATED   | SWU packs, no confirmation                                                           |\n  | `wfx.workflow.dau.phased.yml`           | CREATED → DOWNLOAD → … → ACTIVATED                                     | SWU packs, with confirmation steps                                                   |\n  | `wfx.workflow.dau.download-install.yml` | DOWNLOAD → DOWNLOADING → DOWNLOADED → INSTALL → INSTALLING → INSTALLED | `non-swu` packs. Select it with `workflow_type`: a launch does not choose it for you |\n  | `wfx.workflow.dau.simple.yml`           | DOWNLOAD → INSTALL → INSTALLED or TERMINATED                           | Minimal flow                                                                         |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Every workflow ends in `TERMINATED` on failure. The service registers the four files at startup. `workflow_file` must name the `direct` file, and the other three are looked up next to it by replacing `direct` in the file name (see [Configuration](/docs/platform/updates/reference/configuration)). In hawkBit mode there are no WFX jobs: see [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Next steps [#next-steps]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Cards>\n    <Card title=\"Architecture\" description=\"The components and what runs inside the service.\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/architecture\" />\n\n    <Card title=\"Flows\" description=\"Sequence diagrams of every important operation.\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/flows\" />\n\n    <Card title=\"Publish and roll out an update\" description=\"Do it end to end.\" href=\"/docs/platform/updates/guides/publish-and-roll-out\" />\n  </Cards>\n</div>\n"]
